From d14e02430a118e2d797092f80d0906b7133e3626 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 15:53:38 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 034/206] fix(shorebird): rebuild WrapperAllocator on
 upstream's trampoline machinery

wrapper.{h,cc} was written from their DWARF alone. It turns out the class has
an upstream ancestor: runtime/vm/ffi_callback_metadata.{h,cc}, which does the
same job -- COW-duplicate a stub's code page, slice it into fixed-size landing
pads, keep metadata in a parallel RW mapping. The correspondence is field for
field, including the name stub_page_ and the type
MallocGrowableArray<VirtualMemory*>. Theirs generalizes it to two directions by
lifting upstream's compile-time constants into a runtime Layout and replacing
the metadata free list with a bump cursor. Rule 2 says use upstream's, so this
rewrites against it.

That also re-explains the constants: kPageSize 16384 is upstream's
macOS+arm64 branch, kLandingPadSize 8 is its arm64 kNativeCallbackTrampolineSize,
and rx_mapping_size is RXMappingSize() == 2 * kPageSize -- a derivation, which
is why both directions report 32768 while their shared body sizes differ.

Two bugs, both already solved upstream:

  * Every wrapper page leaked. wrapper_pages_ held bare uwords and the
    destructor was empty. Upstream's destructor deletes the VirtualMemory
    objects, which own their mappings. Proven by measurement, not inspection:
    the new test records the page addresses, destroys the allocator, and asks
    mach_vm_region whether a region still starts there. Deleting the free loop
    puts the test back to red.
  * EnsureStubPage now bounds the stub page tightly and unaligned. The old code
    remapped a fixed rx_mapping_size from the stub's address however short the
    stub was; upstream carries a comment about exactly this reading past the end
    of the code area. The old tests used a stand-in of exactly rx_mapping_size,
    so they could never see it.

The sizeof(WrapperAllocator) == 0x98 assert passed throughout, including with
the leaking uword fields. Size asserts constrain widths, not types.

Four methods had the wrong arity against their DW_TAG_subprogram entries, so
the API is now theirs: AllocateMetadata() (they have no WrapEntryPoint),
MetadataForWrapper(uword), WrapperOfMetadata(void*), MetadataOffset() with no
arguments, plus EnsureStubPage/AllocateWrapperPage/EnsureNextWrapperEntry and
Layout::CodeSize/MappingSize/MappingAlignment. MetadataForWrapper takes no page
because MappingAlignment() lets the page base be recovered by masking; that is
why the alignment exists, and it replaces a linear scan.

offset_of_first_wrapper_in_page_ was set to shared_body_size. Upstream's
identically-named field is code_start - page_start, the stub's offset within
its own OS page. Restoring the old value fails three tests, 2044 assertions in
the round-trip.

The duplication is now verified by running it: allocate, convert metadata to an
entry point, call it, get 246 from a duplicate of testFunction. That covers
DuplicateRX, executability, the offset landing on the copied code, and
AllocateMetadata/WrapperOfMetadata being inverse. The stand-in stub is a real
function, so the source page is genuinely RX -- an RW stand-in only passed
because DuplicateRX's protection ASSERT is compiled out in release. There is no
write-through copy-on-write check anymore: the duplicate is RX, so nothing can
write to it at all.

Not verified: whether landing pads precede or follow the shared body in their
generated stub, and what ContainsEntryPoint's out-parameter means (target is
the reading used here). Both are marked INFERRED in the header. wrapper.cc is
still dead-stripped from gen_snapshot -- 0 symbols, unchanged -- because
nothing calls it yet.

55/55 shorebird unit tests pass; product gen_snapshot still links.

